def autocomplete(root, prefix):
    node = root
    for ch in prefix:
        if ch not in node.children:
            return []              # nothing starts with this prefix
        node = node.children[ch]

    results = []
    def dfs(n, path):
        if n.is_end:
            results.append(prefix + path)
        for ch, child in n.children.items():
            dfs(child, path + ch)

    dfs(node, "")
    return results
```

A production autocomplete would cap the DFS at, say, the top 5-10 results by frequency rather than collecting everything — that usually means each node also stores a popularity count so the DFS can prioritize high-value branches first instead of exploring exhaustively.

